1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,650
Link member position, joints, tool marks and repair records.

2
00:00:05,105 --> 00:00:08,309
Seek preserved outer wood and multiple members.

3
00:00:08,763 --> 00:00:12,237
Consider whether different members came from the same tree.

4
00:00:12,697 --> 00:00:18,158
Position, joints, working traces and repair evidence help determine what a cluster of timber

5
00:00:18,171 --> 00:00:20,256
dates represents.

6
00:00:21,059 --> 00:00:25,382
A: incomplete 1600 ring; complete bark edge.

7
00:00:25,838 --> 00:00:30,805
B: last ring 1580; heartwood only; outer loss unknown.

8
00:00:31,259 --> 00:00:38,068
C: heartwood ends in 1590; five sapwood rings; assume 15–25 in total.

9
00:00:38,522 --> 00:00:42,982
Pause and separate the securely constrained interval from the claims that would require

10
00:00:42,995 --> 00:00:46,132
additional assumptions for each sample.

11
00:00:46,935 --> 00:00:51,850
A: felling in the 1600 growth season, under the stated conditions.

12
00:00:52,306 --> 00:00:57,075
B: felling in or after 1580; no upper bound established.

13
00:00:57,529 --> 00:01:03,694
C: approximately 1605–1615, conditional on the supplied model.

14
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:09,450
The same numerical date can have different implications when outer preservation differs.

15
00:01:09,911 --> 00:01:13,760
Compare the conditions behind each interpretation.

16
00:01:14,563 --> 00:01:17,722
Several boards or beams can come from one tree.

17
00:01:18,184 --> 00:01:21,874
Compare patterns, anatomy and member information.

18
00:01:22,328 --> 00:01:27,032
Distinguish possible same-tree matches from independent dating evidence.

19
00:01:27,486 --> 00:01:32,013
Several members may come from one tree, so their number does not equal the number of

20
00:01:32,026 --> 00:01:34,188
independent trees.

21
00:01:34,991 --> 00:01:37,999
Plot dates together with member locations.

22
00:01:38,454 --> 00:01:41,302
Compare them with proposed building phases.

23
00:01:41,756 --> 00:01:46,381
Ask whether younger timber marks repair, extension or replacement.

24
00:01:46,835 --> 00:01:52,085
A date cluster can suggest a building phase, but that interpretation needs support from

25
00:01:52,099 --> 00:01:54,736
structure and other records.

26
00:01:55,539 --> 00:01:59,244
Reused timber can predate its current structural use.

27
00:01:59,699 --> 00:02:04,502
Inspect redundant joints, tool marks, dimensions and location.

28
00:02:04,962 --> 00:02:07,989
An older date alone does not prove reuse.

29
00:02:08,449 --> 00:02:13,910
An older timber can reflect storage, reuse or surviving earlier fabric.

30
00:02:14,370 --> 00:02:18,980
Compare these explanations rather than selecting one immediately.
